{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Three ways to say it was done",
      "body": "1. **Ser + participle** (passive of action) — the action itself; formal, news, history: *La ciudad fue fundada en 1542.* *El ladrón fue detenido por la policía.* Mostly in the preterite, future and with *ha sido*; rare in the present for single events.\n2. **Estar + participle** (passive of result) — the state after the action: *La puerta está cerrada.* (The door is closed.) *El informe ya está terminado.* No agent.\n3. **Passive se** — the most common in everyday speech when the doer doesn't matter: *Se construyó el puente en 1990. Se venden entradas aquí. Se cerraron las fronteras.*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Ser vs. estar with participles",
      "body": "Compare:\n\n- *La tienda fue cerrada por la policía.* (Someone closed it — an event.)\n- *La tienda está cerrada.* (It's closed now — a state.)\n- *La cena fue preparada por el chef.* / *La cena está preparada.* (Dinner is ready.)\n\nThe participle always agrees in gender and number with the subject: *Las cartas fueron enviadas. Los documentos están firmados.*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Active is often better",
      "body": "Spanish uses the *ser* passive much less than English. Where English says \"I was told\", Spanish says *Me dijeron*. \"He was given a prize\" → *Le dieron un premio*. \"It is said that…\" → *Se dice que…* A person can't be the subject of a passive with *dar, decir, preguntar* etc. as in English."
    }
  ],
